# Consumer UX (Navigator)

Full standards: [CONSUMER_STANDARDS.md](../../CONSUMER_STANDARDS.md). When editing UI, follow these hard rules.

## Wording

- Tone: calm, second person, short sentences. No hype or emojis on transactional screens.
- Tier labels for humans: **Core / Comprehensive / Premier** (not Essential/Plus in UI).
- Cash: **You pay**. Sticky: **Current price** / **New price**.
- Page Apply CTA: **Apply / confirm changes**. Leave-guard: **Save / apply changes**. Discard: **Discard draft**.
- Back links: `‹ Back to {destination}` (capital B).
- Deltas: `+£X/mo` / `−£X/mo` only.

## Money

- Payable totals = cash employee contribution only. **Never** fold BIK tax into you-pay / Apply totals.
- Employer budget = core only; dependants and add-ons are employee-paid.
- Show `/mo` with money. Strikethrough = gross/unsubsidised or previous—not decoration.
- Upgrade hint only if a higher tier’s projected you-pay is **strictly lower** after included extras drop.

## BIK tax

- Side-note only: `+ ~£X tax` + (i). Exemplary/illustrative rate (30%).
- Popover explains **why** tax exists briefly. Never call PMI income-tax-free / salary-sacrifice tax-free here.

## Cover & features

- Tier change keeps extras that remain optional on the target plan.
- Lost-access compares real **access**, not selectedExtra IDs alone. Included-on-higher-tier ≠ “removed”.
- Lost-access UI: add-on-eligible first; tier-only second (“Only available on …”).
- Keep-as-add-on: visible Added state while the step is open.
- Review: Current|After table for changed cover fields only; gain/lose feature lists by access.

## Colour semantics

- Charcoal CTA = primary. Emerald = savings/positive. Amber = caution/upgrade/limited scope. Red = errors only.
